# What does NOT enter the repository. The rule: if a run can recreate it, or it is a secret,
# it is not versioned. Everything else is, including what looks like old documentation:
# the reports in docs/history are the evidence of the project, not leftovers.

# Secrets. Never. Only the .example templates are versioned.
#
# `.env.*` and not a list of names: the list already failed once. A real `.env.server`
# with a live OpenRouter key sat here untracked and one `git add .` from a PUBLIC repo,
# because the patterns named `.env.local` and `.env.*.local` and nobody had thought of
# that one. Deny everything, then allow the two templates by name.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
!.env.server.example

# Interpreter and tooling caches
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*.egg-info/
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.coverage
htmlcov/
build/
dist/

# Virtual environments
.venv/
venv/
env/

# Runtime data (MIRAG_DATA_DIR defaults to ./var in a checkout): outputs, artifacts,
# traces and caches are rewritten on every run and traces grow without a ceiling.
var/
*.log

# Loose backups: if one is needed, make it a commit
*.bak

# OS and editor noise
.DS_Store
.idea/
.vscode/
*.swp

# NOT ignored, on purpose:
#   src/mirag/knowledge/                  the corpus: without it retrieval has nothing to search
#   src/mirag/resources/feature_gains.json  production READS it (the feature gate)
#   benchmarks/results/                   the measurements behind the gate
#   tests/fixtures/                       sample repositories the tests need
#   docs/history/                         the reports are part of the project
